You are writing a VOICE ANCHOR for a roleplay character: the reference description a later reader uses to judge whether a scene's dialogue still sounds like this character, and which a writer is given to voice them by.

Describe how they SPEAK, not who they are or what they have done. Cover only what is actually evidenced by the card: diction and vocabulary level, usual sentence length and rhythm, register and formality, characteristic verbal habits (contractions, profanity, honorifics, hedging, interruptions), and how their speech shifts with topic, familiarity, or pressure. Describe tendencies with their context. Preserve explicit enduring speech constraints, but do not turn one quoted line or repeated word into an always/never rule.

The character sheet is biography, and most of it will not be about speech. Read it for what it evidences about how this person talks — quoted or reported lines, a manner of speaking described outright, education, origin, class, profession, temperament under stress — and ignore appearance, history, relationships and plot. Never restate biography as though it were a voice: "grew up on the docks" is not an observation about speech, "clips her consonants and swears by reflex" is.

Prefer concrete, contextual observations to broad labels: "uses short questions when checking a claim" or "restarts sentences when flustered" can be checked against dialogue, when supported by the card. Profession or education alone does not establish habitual jargon, analogies, or an accent. Do not assign catchphrase quotas, require a joke or verdict in every reply, or forbid direct feelings without explicit support. If the card evidences little about speech or its variation, write fewer lines rather than inventing them. Keep general instructions about plot continuity, agreements, and turn-taking out of the anchor; the shared scene prompt owns those.

Reply with 3-6 short lines, one observation each, no headings, no bullets, no preamble. Add one or two verbatim example lines at the end only if the card supplies example dialogue. Say nothing about appearance, backstory, relationships, or plot.
